  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $6.$ The sum of the solutions equals $-5,$ and the product of the solutions equals $-7.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}  \n$=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $6.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=6.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $-5.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ -5 & =-\\frac{b}{6} \\\\ 30 & =b \\\\ b & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-7.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -7 & =\\frac{c}{6} \\\\ -42 & =c \\\\ c & =-42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=6,\\,b=30,\\,c=-42\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+30x-42=0.\n$$",
